# Line endings decide this app's runtime version.
#
# `runtimeVersion.policy` is "fingerprint", and @expo/fingerprint hashes some
# of its inputs as raw bytes rather than as parsed content — .gitignore,
# .easignore and eas.json among them. A checkout that writes CRLF produces a
# different hash for the same file, and therefore a different runtime version.
#
# That failure is silent and total. EAS Update serves an update only to a build
# whose runtime version matches it exactly; a mismatch is not an error anywhere,
# it is an update that never arrives. It has already happened here: one release
# was built from a working copy with .gitignore as LF and .easignore and
# eas.json as CRLF, a state nothing in Git records, and reproducing it was the
# only way to publish an update that binary would accept.
#
# So the working copy is pinned. With these rules the bytes on disk are the
# bytes in the object store, on every machine and after every checkout, and the
# fingerprint depends on the project rather than on who cloned it.
#
# Adding a file that feeds the fingerprint? The rule below already covers it.
# Removing this file re-opens the whole problem.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Never translated, and never diffed as text. The icon and the splash mark are
# fingerprint inputs too, by content hash — a byte changed in one of these
# would move the runtime version as surely as a line ending.
*.png binary
*.mp3 binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary

# Named explicitly although `* text=auto eol=lf` already covers them. These
# three are the raw-byte inputs to the fingerprint, and someone narrowing the
# rule above should have to delete these lines deliberately rather than
# discover the consequence in a release that quietly stops updating.
.gitignore text eol=lf
.easignore text eol=lf
eas.json   text eol=lf
